1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Are you using volume slash market profile analysis

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:05,280
in your futures trading?

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:07,660
If so, you need to watch this video.

4
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:10,339
Volume profile can be a useful tool,

5
00:00:10,339 --> 00:00:12,679
but there's also some serious problems

6
00:00:12,679 --> 00:00:14,580
with the underlying theories

7
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,199
behind this popular analysis method.

8
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:19,420
Keep in mind, volume profile was developed

9
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:22,960
over 30 years ago, like it's older than me,

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:25,920
and I'm getting quite a few gray hairs on my face here.

11
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:27,320
Our understanding of markets

12
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:30,480
has evolved considerably since that time.

13
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:33,320
Now that doesn't make volume profile useless,

14
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:34,600
far from it.

15
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,320
A few tweaks based on our modern understanding

16
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:41,479
of markets can make volume profile a powerful tool.

17
00:00:41,479 --> 00:00:43,380
So, are you ready?

18
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:44,280
Let's get started.

19
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:51,670
Market profile is a form of analysis

20
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:55,850
pioneered in the 80s by J. Peter Steitelmeier,

21
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:58,310
and I have on good authority

22
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:00,609
that that guy is a total boss.

23
00:01:00,609 --> 00:01:03,929
Like I asked around and everybody that traded with him

24
00:01:03,929 --> 00:01:07,170
says he is one of the smartest people they ever met.

25
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:09,209
Then one of his students, John Dalton,

26
00:01:09,209 --> 00:01:11,650
came along with his book, Mind Over Markets,

27
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:15,409
which really popularized the idea among retail traders.

28
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:17,829
Instead of your typical stock chart,

29
00:01:17,829 --> 00:01:20,370
a market profile displays the market

30
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:22,349
as more of a histogram

31
00:01:22,349 --> 00:01:25,689
to show how much time is spent at each price.

32
00:01:25,689 --> 00:01:28,250
The trading day is split up into 30-minute sections

33
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:30,129
called time price opportunities

34
00:01:30,129 --> 00:01:34,530
with each TPO being assigned a letter or a color.

35
00:01:34,530 --> 00:01:38,769
The distribution then shows which TPO's touched that price.

36
00:01:38,769 --> 00:01:41,049
It's more than just a really cool looking chart though.

37
00:01:41,049 --> 00:01:43,049
The resulting graph usually shows

38
00:01:43,049 --> 00:01:45,810
some sort of a Gaussian distribution,

39
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:49,290
which you would not expect in a purely random market.

40
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:51,730
The center of that distribution where the volume is highest

41
00:01:51,730 --> 00:01:55,010
is called the point of control or POC.

42
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:58,650
You can further include the volume around that POC

43
00:01:58,650 --> 00:02:01,010
until you have 70% of the day's volume

44
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:04,769
to define the value area of the day.

45
00:02:04,769 --> 00:02:06,450
Now, as the data evolved,

46
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:07,950
we gained the ability to create

47
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:11,689
the same sort of histogram using a volume instead,

48
00:02:11,689 --> 00:02:13,849
and the resulting volume profile

49
00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:15,870
allows the same sort of analysis,

50
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:18,050
but on more granular data.

51
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,610
Some traders may prefer one over the other,

52
00:02:20,610 --> 00:02:23,090
but volume profile and market profile

53
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:24,729
are largely interchangeable.

54
00:02:24,729 --> 00:02:27,969
So yeah, don't let me confuse you if I mix terms around.

55
00:02:27,969 --> 00:02:30,930
I'll probably say volume profile most of the time,

56
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:32,330
but same difference.

57
00:02:32,330 --> 00:02:33,590
They're all based around

58
00:02:33,590 --> 00:02:37,129
the same underlying theory of markets.

59
00:02:37,129 --> 00:02:41,250
That theory at the heart of market profile analysis

60
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:43,289
is auction theory,

61
00:02:43,289 --> 00:02:46,729
a theory for how markets move relative to value.

62
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:48,650
John Dalton describes this process

63
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:50,530
in the following manner in his book.

64
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:53,389
If the open is considered below value,

65
00:02:53,389 --> 00:02:56,150
price auctions higher in search of sellers.

66
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:59,830
If the open is considered too high by market participants,

67
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:02,629
price auctions lower, searching for buyers.

68
00:03:02,629 --> 00:03:04,250
Once a buyer enters the market,

69
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:06,349
price begins to auction upward

70
00:03:06,349 --> 00:03:08,389
until the last buyer has bought.

71
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:10,810
Similarly, the market auctions downward

72
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:12,729
until the last seller has sold,

73
00:03:12,729 --> 00:03:15,430
constantly searching for information.

74
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:17,270
So under this theory,

75
00:03:17,270 --> 00:03:20,509
when market participants agree on fair value,

76
00:03:20,509 --> 00:03:22,490
the market will stay in a range

77
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:24,090
of creating a distribution.

78
00:03:24,090 --> 00:03:25,490
If fair value changes,

79
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:27,610
the market will auction up or down

80
00:03:27,610 --> 00:03:29,650
in what is called range extension,

81
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:32,610
just like those auctions you see in movies.

82
00:03:32,610 --> 00:03:35,169
Like Wreck-It Ralph 2 has a great example.

83
00:03:35,169 --> 00:03:36,490
They keep bidding it up

84
00:03:36,490 --> 00:03:38,490
until they realize how stupid they are.

85
00:03:38,490 --> 00:03:40,389
Same thing happens in real markets,

86
00:03:40,389 --> 00:03:42,810
but it auctions both ways at the same time.

87
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:44,449
If a new fair value is found,

88
00:03:44,449 --> 00:03:47,169
the market will create a new distribution,

89
00:03:47,169 --> 00:03:50,009
or that range extension move can be rejected,

90
00:03:50,009 --> 00:03:53,449
forcing a test of the other side of the original range.

91
00:03:53,449 --> 00:03:55,530
Most market profile practitioners

92
00:03:55,530 --> 00:03:56,930
will then use this framework

93
00:03:56,930 --> 00:03:59,129
together with past point of control,

94
00:03:59,129 --> 00:04:01,229
value areas and related statistics

95
00:04:01,229 --> 00:04:03,650
to try and predict where the market may go

96
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:04,969
during the trading day

97
00:04:04,969 --> 00:04:08,409
and what prices they're interested in executing at.

98
00:04:08,409 --> 00:04:11,050
So there's a lot to like about volume profile.

99
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:13,530
I feel like a lot of market philosophies

100
00:04:13,530 --> 00:04:15,930
are based on conspiracy theories.

101
00:04:15,930 --> 00:04:18,670
Maybe we'll talk about one of those next video.

102
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:19,930
But that's not the case

103
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:21,689
with auction theory.

104
00:04:21,689 --> 00:04:24,129
It was created by serious market practitioners

105
00:04:24,129 --> 00:04:27,209
based on real observations of the market.

106
00:04:27,209 --> 00:04:28,970
Everyone that has traded seriously

107
00:04:28,970 --> 00:04:32,170
can see it even if they can't quite put it into words.

108
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:34,310
Sometimes the market is dead

109
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:35,850
and you're bored out of your mind

110
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:39,449
and sometimes the trend just never ceases.

111
00:04:39,449 --> 00:04:43,490
Volume profile also tries to utilize data and statistics

112
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:45,209
to create a consistent system.

113
00:04:45,209 --> 00:04:47,649
You can get pretty far with some consistency

114
00:04:47,649 --> 00:04:49,290
and smart risk management.

115
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:52,810
Something volume profile educators will emphasize a lot.

116
00:04:52,810 --> 00:04:55,529
The edge maybe isn't so much in the setup

117
00:04:55,529 --> 00:04:57,410
as it is in being consistent.

118
00:04:57,410 --> 00:04:58,949
But in my opinion,

119
00:04:58,949 --> 00:05:01,889
this is where the cracks in the theory start to appear,

120
00:05:01,889 --> 00:05:04,990
especially when it comes down to some of the statistics.

121
00:05:04,990 --> 00:05:07,870
Many stats end up being useless

122
00:05:07,870 --> 00:05:11,129
due to risk reward and the fat tailed nature of markets.

123
00:05:11,129 --> 00:05:13,889
Even if you have the thousands of samples required

124
00:05:13,889 --> 00:05:16,250
for a statistically significant result,

125
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:20,290
a simple percentage or average is not going to be enough

126
00:05:20,290 --> 00:05:23,449
to understand what's going on or build a strategy.

127
00:05:23,449 --> 00:05:25,930
So why not just back test it?

128
00:05:25,930 --> 00:05:28,170
Well, that's what I always say.

129
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:31,129
In this case though, people have a decent excuse.

130
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:33,529
Generating volume profile requires you

131
00:05:33,529 --> 00:05:35,709
