that we haven't explained in particular.

269
00:11:01,090 --> 00:11:03,450
Why do we get those Gaussian distributions?

270
00:11:03,450 --> 00:11:05,649
There is an explanation.

271
00:11:05,649 --> 00:11:10,590
So let's get into a more modern understanding of markets.

272
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:12,950
The new theory that replaces auction theory

273
00:11:12,950 --> 00:11:14,490
is what many of us refer to

274
00:11:14,490 --> 00:11:17,809
as the order flow-driven view of markets.

275
00:11:17,809 --> 00:11:20,129
Its premise is simple.

276
00:11:20,129 --> 00:11:22,169
Orders move markets.

277
00:11:23,250 --> 00:11:24,250
Is your mind blown yet?

278
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:27,129
Okay, I know that sounds a little overly simplistic,

279
00:11:27,129 --> 00:11:29,549
but working from this simple premise

280
00:11:29,549 --> 00:11:33,370
can reveal big secrets about the market.

281
00:11:33,370 --> 00:11:34,289
So stick with me.

282
00:11:34,289 --> 00:11:36,970
Under this theory, a strong move up or down

283
00:11:36,970 --> 00:11:38,610
is simply the result of orders

284
00:11:38,610 --> 00:11:42,850
and does not necessarily represent some change

285
00:11:42,850 --> 00:11:45,649
in the perception of value among market participants.

286
00:11:45,649 --> 00:11:48,169
Many of the largest players in the markets,

287
00:11:48,169 --> 00:11:51,529
like index funds, are largely price insensitive.

288
00:11:51,529 --> 00:11:53,850
They need to buy or sell a certain amount

289
00:11:53,850 --> 00:11:56,570
to rebalance their fund for the end of the month

290
00:11:56,570 --> 00:11:58,129
or the end of the quarter.

291
00:11:58,129 --> 00:12:01,450
When they buy or sell, it will move the market

292
00:12:01,450 --> 00:12:04,629
despite there being no real change to fair value.

293
00:12:04,629 --> 00:12:06,570
I go into this idea more in my video

294
00:12:06,570 --> 00:12:08,370
on the inelastic theory of markets,

295
00:12:08,370 --> 00:12:09,409
so you can head over there

296
00:12:09,409 --> 00:12:12,809
if you want to learn more about the research behind that.

297
00:12:12,809 --> 00:12:13,909
But there's some other research

298
00:12:13,909 --> 00:12:16,210
I want to get into in this video.

299
00:12:16,210 --> 00:12:19,529
So if price movements do not necessarily reflect changes

300
00:12:19,529 --> 00:12:22,129
in the perception of value among participants,

301
00:12:22,129 --> 00:12:24,129
then why does the market switch

302
00:12:24,129 --> 00:12:26,990
between trending and range bound regimes?

303
00:12:26,990 --> 00:12:28,889
This is where I feel that the ideas

304
00:12:28,889 --> 00:12:31,490
behind auction theory really fail.

305
00:12:31,490 --> 00:12:33,970
People will say that a lot of volume there

306
00:12:33,970 --> 00:12:35,370
indicates interest.

307
00:12:35,370 --> 00:12:39,009
But if a price represents fair value,

308
00:12:39,009 --> 00:12:41,330
why would the majority of the volume trade there?

309
00:12:41,330 --> 00:12:43,490
You don't trade when you think the price is fair,

310
00:12:43,490 --> 00:12:45,889
you trade when you think it's too high or too low.

311
00:12:45,889 --> 00:12:48,350
And this is where the research gets really interesting

312
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:49,769
because it kind of suggests

313
00:12:49,769 --> 00:12:51,529
that it can happen by accident.

314
00:12:51,529 --> 00:12:53,009
So to better understand this,

315
00:12:53,009 --> 00:12:54,850
let's simplify the market a little

316
00:12:54,850 --> 00:12:59,009
by looking at what is called a Walrasian auction.

317
00:12:59,009 --> 00:13:00,629
And if you think anything like me,

318
00:13:00,629 --> 00:13:03,570
no, this is not an auction with Walruses.

319
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:07,730
It's named after a French guy named Leon Walras,

320
00:13:07,730 --> 00:13:09,370
who was, am I saying that right?

321
00:13:09,370 --> 00:13:10,950
Listen, it's French.

322
00:13:10,950 --> 00:13:12,570
Anyways, this was an interesting guy,

323
00:13:12,570 --> 00:13:14,690
but I got to focus here, okay?

324
00:13:14,690 --> 00:13:15,649
Stay on topic.

325
00:13:15,649 --> 00:13:18,610
A Walrasian auction is the type of auction used

326
00:13:18,610 --> 00:13:20,330
in the Taiwan stock market.

327
00:13:20,330 --> 00:13:22,929
So participants can only place limit orders

328
00:13:22,929 --> 00:13:25,470
and the orders are all collected for five minutes.

329
00:13:25,470 --> 00:13:28,370
And then at the end of that time, an auction occurs

330
00:13:28,370 --> 00:13:29,830
and the orders are matched up.

331
00:13:29,830 --> 00:13:33,210
And having those set intervals makes it much easier

332
00:13:33,210 --> 00:13:35,570
to model the dynamics going on.

333
00:13:35,570 --> 00:13:37,029
And so you might get something

334
00:13:37,029 --> 00:13:38,769
that looks a little bit like this,

335
00:13:38,769 --> 00:13:41,850
and no, that is not supposed to be a diagram

336
00:13:41,850 --> 00:13:43,690
of party pillows, okay?

337
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:46,769
So you get these twin peaks here.

338
00:13:46,769 --> 00:13:47,889
That is not any better.

339
00:13:47,889 --> 00:13:51,649
Okay, let's just, let's move on before YouTube gets upset,

340
00:13:51,649 --> 00:13:52,490
okay?

341
00:13:52,490 --> 00:13:54,730
These are the buys, these are the sells.

342
00:13:54,730 --> 00:13:55,769
Orders are matched up

343
00:13:55,769 --> 00:13:58,490
where the buy and sell orders intersect.

344
00:13:58,490 --> 00:14:02,009
Any orders not matched up will be carried forward

345
00:14:02,009 --> 00:14:03,450
to the next auction.

346
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:06,649
The result is there is less liquidity at current prices

347
00:14:06,649 --> 00:14:09,330
because those orders were already filled

348
00:14:09,330 --> 00:14:10,730
and there's more liquidity

349
00:14:10,730 --> 00:14:13,029
immediately above and below that.

350
00:14:13,029 --> 00:14:15,990
This leads to a situation where it can be difficult

351
00:14:15,990 --> 00:14:19,669
for price to move away from that location.

352
00:14:19,669 --> 00:14:23,509
My favorite way of describing this is that price tends

353
00:14:23,509 --> 00:14:27,789
to settle at where supply and demand are at their minimum.

354
00:14:27,789 --> 00:14:30,149
Clearing a price leaves less liquidity

355
00:14:30,149 --> 00:14:33,250
and makes prices more sensitive to orders.

356
00:14:33,250 --> 00:14:35,230
Outside of that small zone though,

357
00:14:35,230 --> 00:14:37,509
there's a lot of liquidity to push through.

358
00:14:37,509 --> 00:14:39,809
So price just kind of gets stuck

359
00:14:39,809 --> 00:14:42,370
in what I refer to as a liquidity hole.

360
00:14:42,370 --> 00:14:43,990
These dynamics can lead

361
00:14:43,990 --> 00:14:46,590
to certain optimal execution strategies

362
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:48,950
that those big players we talked about earlier

363
00:14:48,950 --> 00:14:50,789
use to their advantage.

364
00:14:50,789 --> 00:14:52,049
For us retail traders,

365
00:14:52,049 --> 00:14:55,230
a liquidity hole just absolutely sucks

366
00:14:55,230 --> 00:14:57,509
because nobody likes sitting in a trade

367
00:14:57,509 --> 00:14:59,830
that goes nowhere for an hour.

368
00:14:59,830 --> 00:15:02,490
The big players though can take advantage of it

369
00:15:02,490 --> 00:15:05,230
or even create them intentionally.

370
00:15:05,230 --> 00:15:07,049
So the number of contracts or shares

371
00:15:07,049 --> 00:15:08,590
that they need to buy or sell

372
00:15:08,590 --> 00:15:10,669
is more than the available liquidity.

373
00:15:10,669 --> 00:15:12,110
So to execute,

374
00:15:12,110 --> 00:15:14,710
they have to split up their larger order

375
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:17,429
and execute it in smaller pieces over time.

376
00:15:17,429 --> 00:15:20,029
We call this big order a meta order,

377
00:15:20,029 --> 00:15:21,830
a big order that is split up

378
00:15:21,830 --> 00:15:23,669
and executed in smaller bits.

379
00:15:23,669 --> 00:15:27,669
The execution of large meta orders can take a long time

380
00:15:27,669 --> 00:15:30,309
and this leads to persistent imbalances

381
00:15:30,309 --> 00:15:34,070
between buy and sell orders resulting in trends.

382
00:15:34,070 --> 00:15:35,870
So here's what these guys can do.

383
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:39,909
They will execute until they create a liquidity hole.

384
00:15:39,909 --> 00:15:41,970
They then scale back the aggressiveness

385
00:15:42,809 --> 00:15:44,289
of their orders and execute within that hole.

386
00:15:44,289 --> 00:15:46,370
They're executing more slowly

387
00:15:46,370 --> 00:15:49,169
but price stays there for a long time.

388
00:15:49,169 --> 00:15:51,769
So they end up executing more volume

389
00:15:51,769 --> 00:15:55,570
in the liquidity hole than they could in other places

390
00:15:55,570 --> 00:15:57,929
and that helps them get a better price.

391
00:15:57,929 --> 00:16:00,850
Hence why these liquidity holes often become

392
00:16:00,850 --> 00:16:04,490
point of controls or volume nodes on the volume profile.

393
00:16:04,490 --> 00:16:06,769
And that's why we get these situations

394
00:16:06,769 --> 00:16:08,809
where price will get stuck in a range

395
00:16:08,809 --> 00:16:11,889
and situations where we'll break out into a trend

396
00:16:12,809 --> 00:16:13,970
and it tends to come in waves.

397
00:16:13,970 --> 00:16:16,970
It's a consequence of how liquidity works

398
00:16:16,970 --> 00:16:19,610
rather than something to do with fair value.

399
00:16:19,610 --> 00:16:21,769
If too much liquidity is cleared at once,

400
00:16:21,769 --> 00:16:24,409
it can result in one of these distributions.

401
00:16:24,409 --> 00:16:27,210
This could be from a large transaction happening there

402
00:16:27,210 --> 00:16:29,610
or from an impulsive sweeping move.

403
00:16:29,610 --> 00:16:32,570
This damages the liquidity in that area

404
00:16:32,570 --> 00:16:35,850
and paradoxically causes prices to get stuck.

405
00:16:35,850 --> 00:16:38,090
You ever had that experience where you get in

406
00:16:38,090 --> 00:16:40,809
because the trend is really getting exciting

407
00:16:40,809 --> 00:16:42,889
and as soon as you enter a position,

408
00:16:42,889 --> 00:16:44,129
it starts going nowhere?

409
00:16:44,129 --> 00:16:45,049
This is why.

410
00:16:45,049 --> 00:16:47,409
Once you understand why these areas form,

411
00:16:47,409 --> 00:16:49,649
you can see why it is kind of silly

412
00:16:49,649 --> 00:16:52,529
to ascribe long-term significance to them.

413
00:16:52,529 --> 00:16:54,889
Once the liquidity in that area has healed,

414
00:16:54,889 --> 00:16:57,850
which rough rule of thumb takes about 45 minutes,

415
00:16:57,850 --> 00:16:59,850
that price holds no significance.

416
00:16:59,850 --> 00:17:01,289
Let's pull it all together.

417
00:17:01,289 --> 00:17:04,690
How can we modify our approach to volume profile

418
00:17:04,690 --> 00:17:06,809
to incorporate this new information?

419
00:17:06,809 --> 00:17:09,210
First, realize that the volume profile

420
00:17:09,210 --> 00:17:12,450
is telling you about past liquidity conditions

421
00:17:12,450 --> 00:17:14,369
rather than past value.

422
00:17:14,369 --> 00:17:17,809
So we can use that information to determine better prices

423
00:17:17,809 --> 00:17:20,369
as well as to time our trades.

424
00:17:20,369 --> 00:17:21,450
If I want to buy,

425
00:17:21,450 --> 00:17:23,569
I'd rather do it at the lower end

426
00:17:23,569 --> 00:17:24,769
of the current distribution.

427
00:17:24,769 --> 00:17:26,329
If I'm looking for a trend,

428
00:17:26,329 --> 00:17:29,730
I don't want to enter just as a liquidity hole forms.

429
00:17:29,730 --> 00:17:31,250
I might want to wait a little bit.

430
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:33,049
I think a lot of volume profile traders

431
00:17:33,049 --> 00:17:35,650
may already kind of do this subconsciously.

432
00:17:35,650 --> 00:17:39,049
Now you can make such decisions explicitly

433
00:17:39,049 --> 00:17:43,089
because you have a real understanding of the dynamics

434
00:17:43,089 --> 00:17:44,730
going on under the surface.

435
00:17:44,730 --> 00:17:49,049
Also, since the profile doesn't necessarily show us

436
00:17:49,049 --> 00:17:53,849
anything about value, we need to track that separately.

437
00:17:53,849 --> 00:17:57,849
That means following the news, understanding valuations,

438
00:17:57,849 --> 00:18:00,490
looking at closely related comparable assets,

439
00:18:00,490 --> 00:18:02,769
and keeping track of what we think

440
00:18:02,769 --> 00:18:04,369
was driving past moves.

441
00:18:04,369 --> 00:18:07,650
We want to understand when a move really was driven

442
00:18:07,650 --> 00:18:09,970
by a change in the perception of value

443
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:11,609
and when it was more technical things.

444
00:18:11,609 --> 00:18:13,730
So instead of making theories

445
00:18:13,730 --> 00:18:16,130
about what key levels the market may go to,

446
00:18:16,130 --> 00:18:18,089
I instead recommend making theories

447
00:18:18,089 --> 00:18:22,289
about what flows will drive the market that day.

448
00:18:22,289 --> 00:18:24,890
Flows could be driven by things like breaking news,

449
00:18:24,890 --> 00:18:28,250
but they could also be driven by portfolio rebalancing.

450
00:18:28,250 --> 00:18:30,690
It's not about fundamentals or technicals

451
00:18:30,690 --> 00:18:31,650
or anything like that,

452
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:35,250
but rather how many orders a particular thing

453
00:18:35,250 --> 00:18:36,690
is likely to generate.

454
00:18:36,690 --> 00:18:38,730
So after our analysis,

455
00:18:38,730 --> 00:18:42,490
if we expect an imbalance in those flows,

456
00:18:42,490 --> 00:18:44,490
then we have an opportunity for a trade.

457
00:18:44,490 --> 00:18:46,170
That should give you some ideas

458
00:18:46,170 --> 00:18:49,170
on how to modify your approach to volume profile

459
00:18:49,170 --> 00:18:53,089
to better align with our current understanding of markets.

460
00:18:53,089 --> 00:18:55,730
If you're interested in the empirically-based

461
00:18:55,730 --> 00:18:57,410
order flow view of markets,

462
00:18:57,410 --> 00:18:59,769
make sure to check out my other videos,

463
00:18:59,769 --> 00:19:02,210
particularly the order flow video series

464
00:19:02,210 --> 00:19:03,650
where we go into all of that.

465
00:19:03,650 --> 00:19:06,529
I also have a free strategy PDF

466
00:19:06,529 --> 00:19:10,970
you can download from speculatorseth.com slash strategy.html.

467
00:19:10,970 --> 00:19:13,849
And this goes into a lot more detail

468
00:19:13,849 --> 00:19:15,809
than a video really allows for.

469
00:19:15,809 --> 00:19:18,569
And of course, I'm always available every morning

470
00:19:18,569 --> 00:19:20,130
during our morning live stream.

471
00:19:20,130 --> 00:19:22,170
I stream my entire trading session

472
00:19:22,170 --> 00:19:25,089
and you can come and ask any questions that you want.

473
00:19:25,089 --> 00:19:27,369
So I will see you guys then

474
00:19:27,369 --> 00:19:29,089
every morning at 8.30 Eastern time.

475
00:19:29,089 --> 00:19:34,940
In the meantime, stay excellent team.

476
00:19:34,940 --> 00:19:38,619
Are you using volume profile slash market profile

477
00:19:38,619 --> 00:19:40,779
in your analysis, in your futures trading?

478
00:19:40,779 --> 00:19:42,460
If so, you need to watch this.

